|> Section
    title=Simulating an exponential r.v.

    As an illustration of Simulation Method 4 described above, in the case where $F$ is the $\operatorname{Exp}(\lambda)$ distribution, the function $g$ is easily computed to be $g(p)=-\frac{1}{\lambda} \log(1-p)$, so taking $X=-\frac{1}{\lambda} \log (1-U)$ produces an $\operatorname{Exp}(\lambda)$ r.v. We can simplify this slightly by noting that if $U\sim U[0,1]$ then also $1-U \sim U[0,1]$, so one can use the simpler function $g(1-p)=-\frac{1}{\lambda}\log p$.

    $\textbf{Simulation Method 5.}$ Given a random variable $U\sim U[0,1]$, the random variable $X = -\frac{1}{\lambda}\log U$ has distribution $\operatorname{Exp}(\lambda)$.
